{
  "title": "feat(durable): hide pi-durable's coding tools from the model, honoring allowedTools",
  "state": "closed",
  "merged": true,
  "body": "Refs #356 (non-closing: the pi-action acceptance below still has to run against this ref).\n\n## What changes\n\n- **`hideTools` option** on `createPitDurableExtension` (default `true`). The default hides pi-durable's `CodingTools` names (`read`, `write`, `edit`, `bash`, `powershell`) from the model with pi-durable `wraps` that take `\"model\"` out of `callers`. An array names the tools to hide instead, and `false` hides none. `typescript`, `codemode`, and `tool_search` are never hidden. Model-only and deferred tools are left as they are.\n- **`allowedTools` on pi-durable.** It's read synchronously when the extension is created, from `<agentDir>/pit.json` and, with `trustProject` plus an explicit `cwd`, from `<cwd>/.pi/pit.json`. Precedence and errors match Pi. Config errors, and `trustProject` without `cwd`, are reported through `log.warning`. `src/functions/storage/project.ts` now splits parsing (`parsePitConfig`) from reading, adds a sync reader (`loadPitAllowedToolsSync`), and shares the merge with `loadPitProjectConfig`.\n- **No duplicate tools (added beyond the issue, as requested).** `tools.*` on pi-durable now leaves out the file and shell tools that `workspace.*` and `shell.*` already cover (`read`, `write`, `edit`, `bash`, `powershell`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`), using the same set as on Pi. Programs get one way to read a file or run a command. This differs from the issue's table: hidden coding tools are **not** listed in `pit_tools` and can't be reached through `tools.*`. Other tools (for example `codemode`) can still call them through `executeTool()`. The docs say this moves effects from the host's `ExecutionEnv` to Pit's process. A host with a container or remote env should use `allowedTools` or `hideTools: false` to offer the coding tools.\n- Docs: a new \"Tools the model sees\" section in `docs/durable.md` covering limits, edge cases, and \"not a security boundary\". There's also an Options row, `docs/configuration.md`, and a `CHANGELOG` entry that calls out the default change.\n\n| | Offered to the model | `tools.*` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before | `read, write, edit, bash, typescript` | `read, write, edit, bash` |\n| Default now | `typescript` | other host tools only |\n| `{\"allowedTools\": [\"bash\"]}` (trusted, with `cwd`) | `bash, typescript` | other host tools only |\n\n## Tests\n\n- `test/durable/loadout.test.ts` runs a real Harness with `CodingTools` and Pit, in pi-action's install order. The model's offered tools are read by replaying the request's system-message tool changes. It covers:\n  - a typed table: default, user exception, project replaces, project clears, invalid project (with warning), untrusted project, opt out, explicit list, glob, and never hiding `typescript`;\n  - `trustProject` without `cwd`;\n  - `workspace`/`shell` working while `tools.bash` is unbound and `pit_tools` lists only the other tools;\n  - a hidden tool that another tool can still call through `executeTool()`;\n  - a replacement `bash` from a later extension;\n  - model-only and deferred tools listed in `hideTools`.\n- I removed the old \"reaches the host's coding tools through tools.*\" test because that behavior is gone on purpose.\n- Mutation probes, run by hand because project functions aren't trusted in this runner. All of these break tests: dropping the `allowedTools` exception (4 tests fail), dropping or keeping only `\"model\"` in the `callers` filter (11 and 10 fail), the deferred guard (1 fails), and the model-only guard (1 fails).\n- `npm run check`, the full suite (1827 tests, including `isolation.test.ts`), and coverage all pass.\n\n## Acceptance still to do (AGENTS.md)\n\nPoint a pi-action workflow at `extensions: github:cv/pit#<this branch's sha>` and check:\n1. The first request offers only `typescript`. `pit_tools` lists no coding tools; this is the intended change from the issue's original item 1.\n2. With `.pi/pit.json` set to `{ \"allowedTools\": [\"bash\"] }` and `trust_project: true`, `bash` is offered again. pi-action has to pass `cwd`.\n3. A follow-up on a thread created before the change still works, so a strict provider accepts earlier `read`/`bash` calls that are no longer offered.\n",
